{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "src-08-infinite-radicals-telescoping",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Infinite Radicals and Telescoping Root Patterns",
    "description": "Turn a repeating radical into an equation and make long root sums collapse by rationalising neighbouring terms.",
    "teachingApproach": "Teach two visual structures: self-similarity in an infinite radical and endpoint cancellation in a telescoping root sum. Build from the equation x=√(k+x), practise positive-root selection, derive the neighbouring-root reciprocal identity, then scale from short telescopes to the chapter’s long sum and a mixed capstone.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": "Teach self-similar infinite radicals such as √(1+√(1+…)), √(2+√(2+…)) and √(6+√(6+…)): call the whole value x, replace the repeated tail by x, square, solve the quadratic, and keep the positive value. Then teach the telescoping identity 1/(√(n+1)+√n)=√(n+1)−√n and use it to show why middle terms cancel in the chapter’s long sums. Include an alternating rationalised pattern and the 1/(1+√2)+…+1/(√120+√121) style capstone. Learners should expose the repeated or cancelling structure rather than attempting long direct arithmetic.",
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5, Square Roots and Cube Roots. Approved scope: 30"
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn two root patterns that look endless but are actually short.\n\nOne repeats the same tail forever. The other cancels almost everything in the middle. The trick is to notice the structure before calculating.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "An infinite radical repeats its own tail.\n\nLet:\nx = √(1 + √(1 + √(1 + …)))\n\nThe part after the first 1 is the same whole expression again.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "So replace the repeated tail by x:\n\nx = √(1+x)\nx² = 1+x\nx²−x−1=0\n\nThe positive value is x = (1+√5)/2.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
        "text": "For x = √(1 + √(1 + √(1 + …))), which equation does x satisfy?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "input": {
        "type": "CHOICE",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "label": "x²+x−1=0"
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "label": "x²−x−1=0"
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "label": "x²−1=0"
          },
          {
            "id": "d",
            "label": "x−1=0"
          }
        ]
      },
      "questionReference": {
        "mode": "INLINE",
        "inlineData": {
          "questionType": "PRACTICE",
          "prompt": "For x = √(1 + √(1 + √(1 + …))), which equation does x satisfy?",
